**My Answer:**

Marshalling a `HashSet<string>` to bytes at a very low level (i.e., copying its raw memory layout) is not possible or safe in C#, because `HashSet<T>` is a managed, reference-type collection with a complex internal structure and references to heap objects (the strings). The .NET runtime does not guarantee a stable or public memory layout for such types, and using unsafe code or `Marshal` APIs is not appropriate for this scenario.

The best approach is to serialize the logical contents of the `HashSet<string>` into a byte array, and then deserialize it as needed. Here is a simple, efficient way to do this using manual serialization with UTF-8 encoding:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public static class HashSetStringSerializer
{
    public static byte[] Serialize(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));
        var bytes = new List<byte>();
        bytes.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(set.Count));
        foreach (var s in set)
        {
            if (s == null) throw new InvalidOperationException("Null strings not supported.");
            var strBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(s);
            bytes.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(strBytes.Length));
            bytes.AddRange(strBytes);
        }
        return bytes.ToArray();
    }

    public static HashSet<string> Deserialize(byte[] data)
    {
        if (data == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(data));
        int pos = 0;
        int count = BitConverter.ToInt32(data, pos);
        pos += 4;
        var set = new HashSet<string>();
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            int len = BitConverter.ToInt32(data, pos);
            pos += 4;
            var str = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(data, pos, len);
            pos += len;
            set.Add(str);
        }
        return set;
    }
}
```

**Usage:**
